  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nAn upper level trough extends from Canada southward to California. \nNebraska and Kansas are just ahead of the upper trough with mainly \nsoutheast winds across the forecast area. High temperatures today \nacross south central and central Nebraska and north central Kansas \nwill be hindered by the persistent cloud cover but are expected to \nbe in the upper 50s and 60s. Light rain showers are expected to \ncontinue through the rest of the afternoon into tonight. \nPrecipitation intensity and amounts are expected to increase by late \ntonight across portions of the area. Thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop with the heaviest precipitation under these storms. Rainfall \namounts tonight will generally be a quarter of an inch or less \nexcept in areas of thunderstorms where amounts of around an inch may \noccur. Winds will increase out of the southeast this evening into \ntonight (sustained at around 15 to 25 MPH) as a low-level jet moves \nover the area. The winds along with the persistent cloud cover will \nprevent temperatures from cooling much overnight. In fact, \ntemperatures may warm slightly as the night progresses with \nincreasing wind speeds and heavier rainfall. Lows tonight are \nexpected to be in the 50s and 60s. \n\nThe upper trough/low moves over the central Plains on Monday. The \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected to mainly be out of the \nforecast area by noon on Monday with skies clearing during the \nafternoon. A surface trough will be across the area during the \nafternoon with gusty, southerly winds (sustained at around 15 to 25 \nMPH with gusts possibly up to 40 to 50 MPH in some areas). There is \nsome uncertainty with high temperatures on Monday due to the timing \nof a cold front, strength of the surface trough, and departure \ntiming of the precipitation and cloud cover. Generally think \ntemperatures will warm up into the mid 60s to mid/upper 70s. There \nis a possibility (20% to 30% chance) of temperatures reaching the \nlow 80s in portions of north central Kansas. The timing of the cold \nfront across the forecast area is expected to range from the mid \nafternoon through late evening. Showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to develop along the front during the afternoon into the \nevening hours. Some of these storms may become strong to severe \ndepending on if the atmosphere has enough time to destabilize \nbetween the exiting rain and cloud cover earlier in the day to the \ntiming of the cold front. Tend to think there will be enough \ndestabilization for strong to severe storms to develop. Storm \ndevelopment could be as early as 3 PM and as late as around 7 PM. \nTemperatures Monday night are expected to cool into the 40s and low \n50s. Showers and thunderstorms are expected to linger into Monday \nnight. \n\nThe upper level trough/low will remain across the area on Tuesday \nbut will be a little further east. The cold front will continue \npushing through the area with high temperatures in the 40s and 50s. \nRain showers are expected to continue across portions of the area on \nTuesday. Expect gusty, northerly winds on Tuesday with gusts up to \naround 40 MPH possible in some areas. Low temperatures Tuesday night \nare expected to cool into the 30s and 40s. A slight warm up is \nexpected on Wednesday with drier conditions. High temperatures will \ncontinue to be in the 50s and 60s Thursday through Saturday with low \ntemperatures generally in the 30s and 40s.",
